"Globalizing Capital: A History of the International Monetary System - Third Edition | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg)\nciteturn784view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 247\nL0: cite0†Google Tag Manager†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Image: Cover of Globalizing Capital: A History of the International Monetary System - Third Edition L3: \nL4: Globalizing Capital: A History of the International Monetary System - Third Edition\nL5: \nL6: Barry Eichengreen\nL7: \nL8: Copyright Date: 2019\nL9: \nL10: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg\nL11: \nL12: Edition: NED - New edition, 3\nL13: \nL14: Published by: Princeton University Press\nL15: \nL16: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg\nL17: \nL18: Search for reviews of this book\nL19: \nL20: Book Info\nL21: \nL22: Description\nL23: p strongEssential reading for understanding the international economy—now thoroughly updated/strong Lucid, accessible, and provocative, and now thoroughly updated to cover recent events that have shaken the global economy, emGlobalizing Capital/em is an indispensable account of the past 150 years of international monetary and financial history—from the classical gold standard to today's post–Bretton Woods \"nonsystem.\" Bringing the story up to the present, this third edition covers the global financial crisis, the Greek bailout, the Euro crisis, the rise of China as a global monetary power, the renewed controversy over the international role of the U.S.\nL24: dollar, and the currency war. Concise and nontechnical, and with a proven appeal to general readers, students, and specialists alike, emGlobalizing Capital/em is a must-read for anyone who wants to understand where the international economy has been—and where it may be going./p\nL25: EISBN\nL26: \nL27: 978-0-691-19458-5\nL28: \nL29: Subjects Business, Economics\nL30: \nL31: View 