lems, and provides access to international credits in the event of shocks. Nations find it difficult to efficiently exploit the gains from trade and foreign lending in the absence of an adequately functioning international monetary mechanism.\nL121: Whether that mechanism is functioning poorly or well, it is impossible to understand the operation of the international economy without also understanding its monetary system.\nL122: Any account of the development of the international monetary system is also necessarily an...\nL123: \nL124: cite5†XML L125: \nL126:   5. The Gold Standard\nL127: \nL128: 2 The Gold Standard (pp. 5-40)\nL129: \nL130: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.5\nL131: \nL132: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.5\nL133: Many readers will imagine that an international monetary system is a set of arrangements negotiated by officials and experts at a summit conference. The Bretton Woods Agreement to manage exchange rates and balances of payments, which emerged from such a meeting at the Mount Washington Hotel at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, in 1944, might be taken to epitomize the process. In fact, monetary arrangements established by international negotiation are the exception, not the rule.\nL134: More commonly, such arrangements have arisen spontaneously out of the individual choices of countries constrained by the prior decisions of their neighbors and, more generally, by...\nL135: cite6†XML L136: \nL137:   6. Interwar Instability\nL138: \nL139: 3 Interwar Instability (pp. 41-85)\nL140: \nL141: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.6\nL142: \nL143: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.6\nL144: In the previous chapter we saw how the prewar gold standard was supported by a particular set of economic and political circumstances specific to that time and place. Interwar experience makes the same point by counterexample. Sterling, which had provided a focal point for the harmonization of policies, no longer enjoyed a favored position in the world economy. Britain’s industrial and commercial preeminence was past, the nation having been forced to sell off many of its foreign assets during World War I.\nL145: Complementarities between British foreign investment and exports of capital goods no longer prevailed to the extent that they...\nL146: cite7†XML L147: \nL148:   7. The Bretton Woods System\nL149: \nL150: 4 The Bretton Woods System (pp. 86-126)\nL151: \nL152: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.7\nL153: \nL154: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.7\nL155: Even today, nearly half a century after its demise, the Bretton Woods international monetary system remains an enigma. For some, Bretton Woods was a critical component of the postwar golden age of growth. It delivered a degree of exchange rate stability that was admirable when compared with the volatility of the preceding and subsequent periods. It dispatched payments problems, permitting the unprecedented expansion of international trade and investment that fueled the postwar boom.\nL156: Other perspectives on Bretton Woods are less rosy. Ease of adjustment, it is argued, was a consequence rather than a cause of buoyant growth. And the notion...\nL157: \nL158: cite8†XML L159: \nL160:   8. After Bretton Woods\nL161: \nL162: 5 After Bretton Woods (pp. 127-174)\nL163: \nL164: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.8\nL165: \nL166: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.8\nL167: Even more than the reconstruction of the gold standard in 1925 or the restoration of convertibility in 1958, the demise of the Bretton Woods international monetary system in 1973 transformed international monetary affairs. Ever since central banks and governments had been aware of the instrument that came to be known as monetary policy, the stability of the exchange rate had been the paramount goal to which it was directed.\nL168: Monetary policy was used to peg the exchange rate except during exceptional and limited periods of war, reconstruction, and depression. But in 1973 policy was cut loose from these moorings, and...\nL169: cite9†XML L170: \nL171:   9. A Brave New Monetary World\nL172: \nL173: 6 A Brave New Monetary World (pp. 175-215)\nL174: \nL175: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.9\nL176: \nL177: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.9\nL178: Every decade seems exceptionally turbulent and eventful to those who live through it. Even so, those affected by the operation of the international monetary system in the decade from 1997 could reasonably make this claim. The period opened with the Asian crisis, a shattering event for a region accustomed to stability and one in which exchange rates played a central role. Crises in Brazil, Turkey, and Argentina followed ad seriatim.\nL179: The message seemed to be that emerging markets were incapable of managing the explosive combination of capital mobility and political democracy.\nL180: But no sooner had observers reached this unhappy conclusion...\nL181: \nL182: cite10†XML L183: \nL184:   10. A Decade of Crises\nL185: \nL186: 7 A Decade of Crises (pp. 216-246)\nL187: \nL188: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.10\nL189: \nL190: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.10\nL191: The Global Financial Crisis of 2007–8 hit the world like a hammer. This was not the crisis of which the Cassandras had been warning. They were preoccupied by the problem of “global imbalances”—by the gaping U.S. current account deficit and corresponding Chinese surplus.¹ They were worried that the dollar might crash if China curtailed its demand for American treasury bonds and the United States was unable to finance its external deficit.\nL192: The straightforward nature of those imbalances explains why they, and not other less apparent but ultimately more consequential financial fissures, preoccupied observers. The U.S. current account deficit...\nL193: \nL194: cite11†XML L195: \nL196:   11. Conclusion\nL197: \nL198: 8 Conclusion (pp. 247-252)\nL199: \nL200: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.11\nL201: \nL202: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.11\nL203: The period since the collapse of the Bretton Woods System in the early 1970s saw an initially slow but then accelerating shift away from the earlier regime of pegged-but-adjustable exchange rates. As late as 1970, the idea of floating the exchange rate was almost unheard of, except as a temporary expedient in extraordinary circumstances. But by 1990, roughly 15 percent of all countries had moved to floating rates. By 2016, this share had risen to nearly 40 percent.\nL204: The movement away from pegged-but-adjustable rates was especially prominent in the advanced countries. By the early twenty-first century, such intermediate arrangements had...\nL205: cite12†XML L206: \nL207:   12. GLOSSARY\nL208: \nL209: GLOSSARY (pp. 253-260)\nL210: \nL211: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.12\nL212: \nL213: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.12\nL214: \nL215: cite13†XML L216: \nL217:   13. REFERENCES\nL218: \nL219: REFERENCES (pp. 261-276)\nL220: \nL221: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.13\nL222: \nL223: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.13\nL224: \nL225: cite14†XML L226: \nL227:   14. INDEX\nL228: \nL229: INDEX (pp. 277-298)\nL230: \nL231: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.14\nL232: \nL233: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.14\nL234: \nL235: cite15†XML L236: \nL237:   15. Back Matter\nL238: \nL239: Back Matter (pp. 299-299)\nL240: \nL241: https://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctvd58rxg.15\nL242: https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctvd58rxg.15\nL243: \nL244: cite16†XML L245: \nL246: cite17†Image: Princeton University Press logo "
